`File: force-app/main/default/classes/ErpCustomerSync.cls`
```apex
/**
 * Sends new Accounts to the ERP:  POST callout:ERP/api/customers
 *
 * One HTTP request is sent per Account (as requested, so the ERP team can trace every
 * account on its own). Each request wraps exactly one customer in the JSON array the
 * API expects: [{"salesforceId": "001...", "name": "Acme GmbH", "country": "Germany"}]
 *
 * Why a Queueable instead of a callout inside AccountTrigger (the one deviation):
 *  - The insert that fired the trigger is uncommitted DML; a callout in that transaction
 *    throws "You have uncommitted work pending". Async Apex runs in its own transaction.
 *  - One transaction may make at most 100 callouts (and 120 s of callout time); a
 *    marketplace batch of 200 accounts would need 200 requests.
 *  - The trigger has a 10 s synchronous limit; ERP latency belongs in async context.
 *
 * The trigger therefore starts exactly ONE queueable job. That job sends up to
 * CALLS_PER_JOB requests and, if accounts remain, chains a follow-up job for them, so
 * no transaction gets close to the callout limits. 200 accounts = 4 jobs in the chain,
 * which is within the platform limit for chained queueable jobs.
 *
 * Error handling and retries are out of scope for this ticket: failures are only logged.
 */
public with sharing class ErpCustomerSync implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    // Requests (= callouts) sent by a single job. Kept small so that every job stays well
    // below the 100-callout / 120 s limits and the queueable execution time.
    private static final Integer CALLS_PER_JOB = 50;

    // Salesforce limit for chained queueable jobs; 5 jobs x 50 = 250 accounts per insert.
    private static final Integer MAX_JOBS_IN_CHAIN = 5;

    private static final String ENDPOINT = 'callout:ERP/api/customers';
    private static final Integer TIMEOUT_MS = 30000;

    // Pre-built request bodies: keeps the serialized queueable state small.
    private final List<String> requestBodies;

    // 0-based position of this job within the chain started by the trigger.
    private final Integer chainPosition;

    public ErpCustomerSync(List<String> requestBodies, Integer chainPosition) {
        this.requestBodies = requestBodies;
        this.chainPosition = chainPosition;
    }

    /**
     * Called from AccountTrigger (after insert). Builds the payloads and starts a single
     * async job, whether the insert holds one account (user creating a record) or up to
     * 200 accounts (marketplace integration).
     */
    public static void enqueue(List<Account> newAccounts) {
        if (newAccounts == null || newAccounts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<String> bodies = new List<String>();
        for (Account acc : newAccounts) {
            bodies.add(buildBody(acc));
        }

        System.enqueueJob(new ErpCustomerSync(bodies, 0));
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        Integer callsThisJob = Math.min(CALLS_PER_JOB, requestBodies.size());
        Http http = new Http();

        for (Integer i = 0; i < callsThisJob; i++) {
            sendOne(http, requestBodies[i]);
        }

        if (callsThisJob < requestBodies.size()) {
            List<String> remaining = new List<String>();
            for (Integer i = callsThisJob; i < requestBodies.size(); i++) {
                remaining.add(requestBodies[i]);
            }

            if (chainPosition + 1 < MAX_JOBS_IN_CHAIN) {
                System.enqueueJob(new ErpCustomerSync(remaining, chainPosition + 1));
            } else {
                System.debug(
                    LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'ErpCustomerSync: ' + remaining.size() + ' account(s) not sent to the ERP. '
                        + 'More than ' + (MAX_JOBS_IN_CHAIN * CALLS_PER_JOB)
                        + ' accounts in one transaction requires Batch Apex.'
                );
            }
        }
    }

    /** Sends the payload of one account; logs failures and continues with the next one. */
    private void sendOne(Http http, String body) {
        HttpRequest request = new HttpRequest();
        request.setEndpoint(ENDPOINT);
        request.setMethod('POST');
        request.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
        request.setTimeout(TIMEOUT_MS);
        request.setBody(body);

        try {
            HttpResponse response = http.send(request);
            if (response.getStatusCode() != 202) {
                System.debug(
                    LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'ErpCustomerSync: ERP returned ' + response.getStatusCode()
                        + ' (' + response.getBody() + ') for payload ' + body
                );
            }
        } catch (CalloutException e) {
            // Retries are out of scope: log and continue so the remaining accounts
            // are still pushed.
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR, 'ErpCustomerSync: callout failed for ' + body + ': ' + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    /** Builds the ERP payload for one account: [{"salesforceId":"001..","name":"..","country":".."}] */
    private static String buildBody(Account acc) {
        String country = String.isBlank(acc.BillingCountry) ? null : acc.BillingCountry;

        Map<String, Object> customer = new Map<String, Object>();
        customer.put('salesforceId', String.valueOf(acc.Id)); // Ids in Apex are always the 18-character form
        customer.put('name', acc.Name);
        customer.put('country', country);

        return JSON.serialize(new List<Object>{ customer });
    }
}
```

The root cause: the 200‑case nightly close can only enqueue **50** Queueables per transaction, and **any** scheme that nests async work (Queueable→Queueable, or `Database.executeBatch` fired from the trigger when the test exercises more than one bulk update) hits the platform limits you saw — `Maximum stack depth` for chained queueables and `No more than one executeBatch…` for batches.

Fix: keep the literal "one `CaseSurveyJob` per case" for everything a transaction can physically enqueue (≤ 50, i.e. normal agent bulk closes), and for the oversized API/nightly close (up to 200 in one transaction) hand the whole set to a **single** `CaseSurveyJob` in "bulk" mode. That bulk job does **no** nested async (so no depth/executeBatch errors) and inserts the tasks with `Database.insert(tasks, false)` so one bad record cannot roll back the rest — preserving the "one failure can't affect the others" intent at the record level. No case is processed twice.

