public with sharing class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable {
    /** Maximum number of Queueable jobs that can be enqueued from one transaction. */
    public static final Integer MAX_JOBS_PER_TRANSACTION = 50;

    private final Id caseId;
    private final List<Id> bulkCaseIds;

    /**
     * Per-case mode: one job per closed case (used for normal bulk closes).
     */
    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseId = caseId;
        this.bulkCaseIds = new List<Id>();
    }

    /**
     * Bulk mode: used only when a single transaction closes more cases than can be
     * enqueued individually (> MAX_JOBS_PER_TRANSACTION). Processes all cases in one
     * job with partial-success inserts so a single bad record cannot affect the rest.
     */
    public CaseSurveyJob(List<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseId = null;
        this.bulkCaseIds = new List<Id>();
        if (caseIds != null) {
            for (Id id : caseIds) {
                if (id != null) {
                    this.bulkCaseIds.add(id);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (caseId != null) {
            createSurveyTask(caseId);
            return;
        }

        if (!bulkCaseIds.isEmpty()) {
            createSurveyTasks(bulkCaseIds);
        }
    }

    private void createSurveyTask(Id surveyCaseId) {
        List<Case> cases = queryCases(new Set<Id>{ surveyCaseId });
        if (cases.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        insert buildTask(cases[0]);
    }

    private void createSurveyTasks(List<Id> surveyCaseIds) {
        List<Case> cases = queryCases(new Set<Id>(surveyCaseIds));
        if (cases.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        for (Case c : cases) {
            tasks.add(buildTask(c));
        }

        // Partial success: one failing record does not roll back the others.
        Database.insert(tasks, false);
    }

    private List<Case> queryCases(Set<Id> ids) {
        return [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id IN :ids
              AND ContactId != null
        ];
    }

    private Task buildTask(Case c) {
        return new Task(
            WhatId = c.Id,
            WhoId = c.ContactId,
            Subject = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber,
            ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(2),
            Status = 'Not Started'
        );
    }
}
```

What changed and why:
- **≤ 50 closed cases:** the trigger loops and does `System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(caseId))`, exactly one job per case, so each case still runs in its own transaction and a failure is isolated. This satisfies the single-case / no-contact / not-closed tests and normal agent list-view closes.
- **> 50 closed cases (nightly 200 via API):** the 50-enqueue limit and the test-context limits on nested async (`Maximum stack depth`, `No more than one executeBatch…`) make per-case enqueuing or batching from the trigger fail. The oversized set is passed to **one** `CaseSurveyJob`, which queries the cases once, builds one task per case, and writes them with `Database.insert(tasks, false)` so a single bad record cannot block the rest. No nested queueables and no batch are launched, so none of the prior limits are hit.
- Cases without a contact are filtered in the trigger and again in the SOQL (`ContactId != null`), so they never get a task. The task fields (`WhatId`, `WhoId`, `Subject`, `ActivityDate` = today + 2, `Status` = `'Not Started'`) match the spec exactly.

